--TEST--
A parameter default takes the same compile-time constant set a property default does, and an omitted enum case arrives as a case rather than as its backing integer — `rule:attributes/payload-is-a-compile-time-constant`, `rule:enums/no-class-machinery`, `rule:enums/truthiness`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `rule:attributes/payload-is-a-compile-time-constant`'s constant set is a literal, another class's `const`, or an
// enum case, and `nvs_types::defaults` folds all three at signature
// collection for a parameter exactly as it does for a property. A parameter's
// constant is then emitted at every call site that omits it, under the
// parameter's own IR type.
enum Mode { Off = 0, Fast = 3 }

class Limits
{
    public const int COUNT = 12;
    public const string NAME = "novis";
}

class Runner
{
    public static function describe(Mode $m = Mode::Fast, int $n = Limits::COUNT, string $tag = Limits::NAME): void
    {
        echo $tag, ":", $m as int, ":", $n, "\n";
    }

    // `rule:enums/truthiness`: a case is truthy whatever integer backs it, so
    // a default of the `0` case is still true in a condition — which the `int`
    // constant `0` would not be. That is the whole of what emitting the
    // default at the parameter's own type buys.
    public static function on(Mode $m = Mode::Off): bool
    {
        if ($m) {
            return true;
        }
        return false;
    }
}

Runner::describe();
// Agreement: the omitted default and the written case are one value.
Runner::describe(Mode::Fast);
Runner::describe(Mode::Off, 1, "x");
if (Runner::on()) {
    echo "a case is truthy\n";
}
--EXPECT--
novis:3:12
novis:3:12
x:0:1
a case is truthy
